    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "\"The Chesapeake and Ohio (C&O) Canal National Historical Park, herein called the C&O Canal, crosses three physiographic provinces along the Potomac River. From Georgetown, District of Columbia, to Cumberland, Maryland, the C&O Canal provides an opportunity to examine the geologic history of the central Appalachian region.\r\n\r\nThe geologic map (Chapter A) depicts the 184.5-mile-long park in a 2-mile wide corridor centered on the Potomac River. The geologic report (Chapter B) is presented east to west, by provinces and sections, -- the Piedmont (including the Potomac terrane, the Culpeper basin, the Westminster terrane, and the Frederick Valley synclinorium), the Blue Ridge, and the Valley and Ridge (including the Great Valley). Geologic features are keyed to the NPS mile markers (MM) that are found along the towpath from Georgetown (MM 0) to Cumberland (MM 184.5).\"",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
    ],
